# Accessibility — Semantic UI Is Correct UI

**Impact: HIGH**

Accessibility is part of component correctness, not optional polish.

## Semantic HTML first

Use the native element that matches the behavior:

- `<button>` for actions;
- `<a>`/router Link for navigation;
- `<label>` for form labels;
- headings in logical hierarchy;
- lists/tables only for list/tabular semantics.

Do not use clickable `div`/`span` elements when a native interactive element exists.

ARIA supplements semantics; it should not recreate native behavior unnecessarily.

## Keyboard access

All interactive behavior **MUST** be operable by keyboard when the underlying interaction is keyboard-operable.

Do not implement mouse-only click, hover, drag, menu, dialog, popover or custom control behavior without an accessible keyboard path.

Prefer shadcn/Radix/native primitives for complex interaction semantics.

## Focus

- Focus indicators **MUST** remain visible for keyboard users.
- Do not globally remove outlines without an equally visible replacement.
- Dialogs/popovers/menus SHOULD use primitives that manage focus appropriately.
- After validation/navigation/dialog actions, focus behavior should remain understandable; move focus intentionally only when it improves the workflow.

## Forms

Every control has a programmatically associated label or appropriate accessible name.

Placeholders are not replacements for labels.

Errors SHOULD identify the affected field and be associated with it when practical. Do not communicate required/error/success state by color alone.

## Images and icons

- Informative images need meaningful alternative text.
- Decorative images/icons use empty/hidden semantics so they are not announced as noise.
- Icon-only buttons need an accessible name.

Do not use file names such as `IMG_1234` as alt text.

## Dynamic content

Loading, errors, toasts and async status changes should be understandable without relying solely on visual animation/color. Use the accessible behavior already provided by the chosen primitive/library where possible.

Do not add aggressive live-region announcements for every minor visual update.

## Motion

Respect reduced-motion preferences for non-essential animation. Do not make critical information available only through animation/hover.

## Testing priority

Accessibility-friendly queries (`getByRole`, label/name/text) are preferred in tests because they verify the same semantics users and assistive technologies depend on.
